//// A primitive method on a lambda's parameter is the same method it is
//// anywhere else. `xs.filter({ e -> e.name.contains("x") })` and
//// `"box".contains("x")` ask for one thing, so they must lower to one thing.
////
//// Measured by emilia's theme front on commonJS, where the lambda form died at
//// run time with `e.name.contains is not a function`. `String.contains` is
//// declared `#[@External.Node("includes")]` in `libs/std/src/primitives.bp`,
//// and the call-site rename that reads that annotation only fires on a
//// receiver the inferencer has resolved to a primitive. A lambda ARGUMENT was
//// inferred with fresh variables for its parameters — nothing pushed the
//// receiver's element type down into it — so inside the lambda the receiver
//// was an unresolved variable, the rename never fired, and commonJS emitted
//// `.contains(…)` verbatim. erlang and wasm lower `contains` off the recorded
//// primitive family instead, so the same source was green on them.
////
//// The direct receiver and the record field outside a lambda are the controls:
//// both always worked, and they are here so a regression that fixes one and
//// breaks the other is visible.
////
//// `startsWith` is a second method with the same annotation shape and no
//// rename (the node symbol equals the botopink name), so it pins that the
//// element type reaching the lambda is what dispatches, not the rename table.

type Item(name: string)

pub fn main() {
    val names = ["box", "cup", "axe"];

    @print("box".contains("x"));

    val it = Item(name: "box");
    @print(it.name.contains("x"));

    @print(names.filter({ s -> s.contains("x") }).join(","));

    @print(names.filter({ s -> s.startsWith("c") }).join(","));

    val items = [Item(name: "box"), Item(name: "cup"), Item(name: "axe")];
    @print(items.filter({ e -> e.name.contains("x") }).length);
}
